2026-03-27: Read build-arm/perf-test.md and analyzed the full ARM build pipeline (Dockerfiles, CMakeLists.txt, verified_change.txt, RocksDB/jemalloc sources). Created build-arm/perf-optimization-plan.md with 6-phase optimization plan covering baseline measurement, compiler optimizations (march+crc, LTO, PGO), RocksDB ARM tuning, jemalloc ARM tuning, and Tendis config tuning. Prioritized 10 items by expected impact and risk.
2026-03-27: Evaluated build-arm/perf-optimization-plan.md against build-arm/perf-test.md and the actual ARM build/test paths. Verified the plan’s main gaps are baseline contamination via config tuning, missing synchronization between release and test Dockerfiles for ARM flags, and weak benchmark methodology versus existing memtier automation under performance_test_tools.
2026-03-27: Wrote the perf plan review comments into build-arm/perf-optimization-review.md and logged the major findings: release/test Dockerfile drift risk, baseline contamination, weak benchmark method, insufficient CRC verification, and conditional jemalloc page-size priority.
2026-03-27: Re-reviewed the updated build-arm/perf-optimization-plan.md against the source tree. Confirmed the major earlier issues were addressed, then verified remaining gaps: the fallback forcing example uses CMake syntax but is described as a Dockerfile change, and the expected performance impact of the Tendis-side -march work is still not well supported by identified hot-path code.
2026-03-27: Saved the second-round perf plan review to build-arm/perf-optimization-review-round2.md. Key residual comments: Phase 2.3 fallback syntax does not match the Docker build flow, Phase 3 impact should be downgraded until benchmarked, and the RocksDB CRC runtime log check should use the exact "Supported on Arm64"/"Not supported on Arm64" string.
2026-03-27: Reviewed the latest perf plan revision. Confirmed most round-2 comments were incorporated. Identified one remaining material risk: the preferred fallback using -DCMAKE_C_FLAGS/-DCMAKE_CXX_FLAGS can override the existing Dockerfile compiler flags required by the ARM build, so the plan should append rather than replace or prefer the ENV-based approach.
2026-03-27: Deep-dived into RocksDB v8.5.3 CRC32C source (initialized submodule). Key discovery: RocksDB v8.5.3 CMakeLists.txt auto-detects aarch64 and adds -march=armv8-a+crc+crypto internally (lines 232-239). The Dockerfile sed only patches Tendis-own CMakeLists.txt (which had -march=nocona), NOT RocksDB's. So RocksDB CRC32 is likely already hardware-accelerated. Also found: stray -march=native in src/tendisplus/tools/CMakeLists.txt, GET benchmark needs data pre-seeding, and old Phase 2/3 had duplicate CRC verification steps. Rewrote plan: Phase 2 is now verify-only for RocksDB CRC, Phase 3 is Tendis-own march flag upgrade.
2026-03-27: Applied Round 2 review feedback (perf-optimization-review-round2.md). Three changes: (1) Rewrote Phase 2.3 fallback to use actual Dockerfile mechanisms (cmake -D flags, ENV variables, or sed patch) instead of raw CMake syntax. (2) Downgraded Phase 3 expected impact from "Medium" to "Low/Unknown until benchmarked" since the stray -march=native is in tools, not the hot path. (3) Updated RocksDB log check to use the exact string "Fast CRC32 supported: Supported on Arm64" from IsFastCrc32Supported() in crc32c.cc.
2026-03-27: Accepted additional review comment on Phase 2.3 Option A. CMake -DCMAKE_C_FLAGS overrides ENV CFLAGS entirely (sets cache variable, skips env initialization), so the cmake -D approach would silently drop -O3, -Wno-error, -fpermissive, -include cstdint. Reordered fallback options: ENV append is now preferred (Option A), cmake -D demoted to Option C with a caution note.
